---
const rows = [
  ["Subject", "Does the action: I, he, she, we, they", "She waved."],
  ["Object", "Receives the action, or follows a preposition: me, him, her, us, them", "Sam saw her."],
  ["Possessive", "Stands for something belonging to someone: mine, yours, hers, ours, theirs", "The red bag is hers."],
  ["Reflexive", "Points back to the subject: myself, himself, herself, ourselves", "She taught herself."]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Pronouns - Referring without repetition</h1>
  <p>
    A <strong>pronoun</strong> stands in for a noun or noun phrase: “<strong>Lucy</strong> found a key.
    <strong>She</strong> kept <strong>it</strong> safe.” <em>She</em> refers to Lucy; <em>it</em> refers to the
    key. The person or thing a pronoun refers to is its <strong>referent</strong>. The reader must be able to
    tell what that referent is.
  </p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-lilac">
    <h2>Jobs of pronouns</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Job</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">Examples</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Sentence</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map(([job, examples, sentence]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{job}</th>
              <td>{examples}</td>
              <td>{sentence}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <p>
    To check a pair, cover the other person. “Maya and <strong>I</strong> went” still works as “<strong>I</strong>
    went.” “Dad drove Maya and <strong>me</strong>” still works as “Dad drove <strong>me</strong>.”
  </p>
  <p>
    “That coat is <strong>mine</strong>” uses a possessive pronoun. “That is <strong>my coat</strong>” uses a
    determiner. <em>His</em> can do both jobs. <strong>Its</strong> means belonging to it; <strong>it’s</strong>
    means <em>it is</em> or <em>it has</em>.
  </p>
  <p class="try-it">
    <strong>Try it.</strong> Choose <em>He/Him</em> in “___ carried the bags,” and <em>we/us</em> in “The
    teacher spoke to ___.” Why could “Ravi told Omar he was late” be unclear?
  </p>
  <div class="trap-card trap-compact">
    <section class="trap">
      <h2>I, me and made-up reflexives</h2>
      <p>
        Me and Maya went.
        <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span>
        Maya and <strong>I</strong> went.
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        He taught hisself.
        <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span>
        He taught <strong>himself</strong>.
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        They hurt theirselves.
        <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Incorrect">✕</span>
        They hurt <strong>themselves</strong>.
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="trap">
      <h2>Make the referent clear</h2>
      <p>
        Ava told Lily she had won.
        <span class="mark mark-no" aria-label="Unclear">✕</span>
        <em>She</em> could be Ava or Lily.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ava told Lily, “You have won.”
        <span class="mark mark-yes" aria-label="Correct">✓</span>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
